Vendor lock-in with proprietary software creates switching costs that give suppliers pricing power — governments and organisations that rely on open source are not subject to unilateral licensing price increases.
Open source accelerates innovation by allowing organisations to build on each other's work rather than reinventing the same wheel behind different proprietary walls.
The smartphone revolution depended entirely on Linux (Android), open source browsers (WebKit, V8), and open protocols (TCP/IP, HTTP) — without these commons, the industry would not exist as we know it.
